    title: "Stories of Resilience",
    text: [
      "A failed exam, a lost job or a sudden accident can make life feel impossible. What matters is how we get up again. Here are three stories of ordinary people with remarkable resilience.",
      "Khalid, a fisherman from Aqaba, had sailed out alone every morning for thirty years. One day his engine died, and by evening he was adrift, carried by the wind far from the coast. He had only two bottles of water and some bread, so he made a rule: one sip at dawn, one at sunset. Each night he scratched a line on the boat to count the days. By the sixth day his lips were cracked and his voice was almost gone. Then he spotted a cargo ship on the horizon. He tied his shirt to an oar and waved it until his arms ached. When the ship slowly turned towards him, tears ran down his face. His ordeal was over.",
      "Salma, a student from Irbid, had always been near the top of her class, so failing two final exams felt like the end of the world. She told her friends she was fine, but she stopped answering their calls and stayed in her room for a week. Then her grandmother, who had never been to school, said, 'A seed that does not grow in one season is not a dead seed.' The next morning Salma opened her old notebooks. She asked her teachers for help, changed her study habits and, a year later, passed with high marks.",
      "Yazan was a promising runner until a knee injury ended his season. The doctor warned that he might never race again, and for weeks he refused to look at his running shoes because they reminded him of everything he had lost. Slowly, however, he began swimming and doing gentle exercises with a physiotherapist. Eighteen months later, he stood at the start of a 1,500-metre race. He did not win, but he was smiling as he crossed the finish line.",
      "The three were very different, yet they shared the same qualities: determination, help from others and the patience to take one small step at a time. If they had given up, their stories would have ended very differently. Setbacks are part of every life; what counts is how we respond."
    ],
    skill: "Inferring meaning and feelings from context",
    skillAr: "استنتاج المعنى والمشاعر من السياق",
    skillTip: "الكاتب لا يخبرك دائماً بما يشعر به الشخص، لذلك راقب تصرفاته وأقواله (tears ran down his face, refused to look at his running shoes) وانتبه للتناقض بين ما يقوله وما يفعله. وإذا قابلت كلمة جديدة فاقرأ الجمل قبلها وبعدها؛ فستجد غالباً شرحاً أو مثالاً أو نتيجة تساعدك على تخمين معناها (مثل adrift: المحرك تعطّل والريح تحمله بعيداً عن الشاطئ).",
